        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $y=x^2+2x-8$ and $y=2x+1$ intersect at $(3,7)$ and $(-3,-5)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The intersections are $(3,7)$ and $(-3,-5)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}x^2+2x-8&=2x+1\\\\x^2-9&=0\\\\(x-3)(x+3)&=0\\\\x&=3\\text{ or }x=-3\\end{aligned}$$\nAt $x=3$, $y=2(3)+1=7$, giving $(3,7)$.\nAt $x=-3$, $y=2(-3)+1=-5$, giving $(-3,-5)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 33
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q9b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Sketch both graphs, shading the enclosed region.",
